WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.519
Every few years, a school district is
celebrated for some eye-catching numbers.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.240
It is held up as a national model... and
then, later, the numbers are unmasked

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.540
as deceptive. In the early '80s,
Atlanta's test scores showed remarkable gains.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.480
The district's secret sauce? Easier tests.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.660
In the '90s, Houston's test gains
were labeled a "Texas miracle."

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:30.580
A 2000 RAND study found these
scores were "misleading" and "inflated."

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:35.520
In the mid-2000s, Chicago was hailed
as a model for "fixing America's schools."

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.200
It turns out that the impressive passage
rates were fueled by lowered standards.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.420
By then, Atlanta was being
celebrated again, and then district

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:49.280
leaders went to jail for cooking test scores.
I could list many more. The big problem?

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.960
Everybody leaps to imitate the
miracle of the moment, which distracts

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.479
from the hard work of
actually improving schools.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.040
Be wary of "models" which are a mélange of
quick fixes and charismatic leadership.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:07.400
So, the next time you're told that
district X has it all figured out, look twice.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.140
Do you think test scores are a
reliable measure of how well school

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.490
districts are doing? Let us know in our
poll. Also, let us know what other topics

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:19.600
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60
seconds and be sure to LIKE and

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.600
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

